 ## 2026-07-20
 
+### 1929 — HEV codec review requires structural validation before limit policy (TASK-260715-516lhy)
+- REVIEW: Changes requested. Both Swift and Go decoders inspect `MSGLEN` against the 1472 protocol ceiling and lowered local cap before completing HEV structural validation. This reverses the accepted TASK-260715-18owh7 §4.4 order (structure at step 4, limits at step 5).
+- CONSEQUENCE: With local cap 512, the truncated IPv4 record `02 01 0A 01 C0 00 02 01 00 35` declares 513 DATA bytes but contains none; both implementations currently return the survivable `messageLengthExceedsLocalMaximum` / `rejectDatagram` policy result instead of the association-fatal structural length failure. Existing tests cover only a structurally valid local-cap record and therefore miss the combined case.
+- REWORK: Validate ATYP/HDRLEN/address/port and exact checked `outerLength == HDRLEN + MSGLEN` before applying the protocol/local ceiling split, then add mirrored Swift/Go tests for malformed-plus-over-limit precedence and a structurally valid 1473-byte protocol violation. Full existing validation remained green, proving this is a coverage/contract gap rather than a build failure. Board evidence: `TASK-260715-516lhy_review.md`.
+- RESOLUTION: Swift and Go now validate ATYP, recomputed HDRLEN, address and port bounds, nonzero port, and exact outer/MSGLEN arithmetic before applying either payload limit. Mirrored tables cover every structural stage at both 513-byte local-policy and 1473-byte protocol-violation declarations with zero decoded materialization; valid 513-byte and 1473-byte records retain their distinct reject-versus-close outcomes. Verification: relay protocol gate, 150 Swift tests, Go vet/tests with 91.3% coverage, 1,135,854 fuzz executions, strict formatting, core-boundary guard, and diff validation all pass.
+
+### 1920 — HEV UDP payload codec is byte-exact and allocation-bounded (TASK-260715-516lhy)
+- IMPLEMENTATION: Swift `RelayDatagramCodec` and Go `DatagramCodec` recompute the unsigned big-endian `MSGLEN`, one-byte `HDRLEN`, ATYP-specific address width, nonzero port, DATA length, and total envelope-payload length before any decoded slice or allocation. Exact encoder sizing precedes one bounded record allocation; the largest legal record is the generated 1727-byte bound.
+- DECISION: The codec preserves HEV domain bytes as an opaque 1–248-byte wire value, including non-ASCII and NUL. DNS presentation-form, invalid A-label, raw-IP-text, and IDNA policy remains exclusively in the later Go resolution gate; neither codec normalizes, resolves, or logs domain bytes. The endpoint type is direction-neutral so a response encodes the relay-observed source rather than substituting the original destination.
+- ERROR BOUNDARY: Invalid local configuration is session-fatal; invalid locally supplied encoder input is `rejectDatagram`; a malformed peer record under a trusted nonzero association closes that association; a valid record above a lowered local cap is dropped as `rejectDatagram`. Errors expose only finite code/phase/scope/disposition fields.
+- VERIFICATION: Both languages pass the same literal IPv4, IPv6, and domain HEV layouts at zero, typical, and 1472-byte DATA; property tests cover all 1473 payload sizes for all address forms. Truncation/length mutation tests prove zero decoded materialization on failure. Go allocation checks cap encode/decode at 1/2 allocations, 5-second fuzzing executed 307,263 cases, and package coverage reached 91.0%.
+
 ### 1847 — Incremental relay envelope codec is bounded and cross-language equivalent (TASK-260715-89h7cw)
 - IMPLEMENTATION: Swift and Go now encode the four-byte big-endian body length plus type/flags/association ID as separate header/payload write slices, and decode arbitrary partial/coalesced stream reads while retaining at most one negotiated frame plus the fixed prefix. Prefix bounds are checked before the first body allocation; generated message metadata drives direction, association-ID, fixed-payload, reserved-flag, and negotiated DNS-priority validation.
 - DECISION: One `consume` call is transactional when a later coalesced frame is session-fatal: frames completed earlier in that same call are not returned or counted, while frames returned by earlier calls remain counted. This keeps the throwing Swift API and mirrored Go behavior deterministic and preserves `inputBytes = outputBytes + retainedBytes + discardedBytes` after both success and failure.
